"What prompt should I have used from the very start, and what would have been the most useful questions to ask?" 

This is a powerful, highly structured framework for extracting deep, nuanced insights from an AI model. By treating the conversation as a multi-stage research process rather than a single search query, "The Seven Moves" prevents vague or surface-level AI outputs.

Here is a quick analysis of why this strategy works so well and how to get the most out of each move:

Why "The Seven Moves" Strategy Works
Bypasses Surface Summaries (Moves 1 & 2): Framing the topic around debate or genuine stakes forces the model to present balanced perspectives rather than a generic textbook definition. Following up with research and time-horizon queries forces evidence-based reasoning over hype.

Turns the Model into an Expert Collaborator (Moves 3 & 4): AI often excels when asked to generate questions or formulate hypotheses. Moves 3 and 4 flip the dynamics—instead of you asking all the questions, you prompt the AI to reveal blind spots and non-obvious angles you might not have considered.

Forces Epistemic Honesty (Move 5): Asking for confidence ratings (High/Medium/Low) and underlying assumptions forces the model to self-evaluate, helping you identify where the consensus is strong versus where it is speculative.

Ensures Synthesis & Meta-Learning (Moves 6 & 7): Creating a single synthesized prompt allows for cross-model validation (e.g., comparing answers across different AI systems). The retrospective in Move 7 acts as a feedback loop to refine your prompt engineering skills for future research.

Pro-Tip for Execution
When running these moves, keep your initial {topic} specific. Instead of asking about broad topics like "artificial intelligence", narrow it down to actionable questions like "the impact of generative AI on high-school essay assessment."

